## 8. Validate the Docker Compose File

Move to the project directory:

```bash
cd /opt/sonarqube
```

Validate the configuration:

```bash
docker compose config
```

The command should display the completed configuration without errors.

Pull the container images:

```bash
docker compose pull
```

---

## 9. Start SonarQube

Start the containers:

```bash
docker compose up -d
```

Check their status:

```bash
docker compose ps
```

You should eventually see both containers running:

```text
sonarqube
sonarqube-db
```

Monitor SonarQube startup:

```bash
docker compose logs -f sonarqube
```

Press `Ctrl+C` to stop viewing the logs. This does not stop the container.

Monitor PostgreSQL logs when needed:

```bash
docker compose logs -f db
```

---

## 10. Configure the Firewall

Open SonarQube TCP port `9000`:

```bash
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=9000/tcp
firewall-cmd --reload
```

Verify:

```bash
firewall-cmd --list-ports
ss -lntp | grep 9000
```

Expected firewall output should include:

```text
9000/tcp
```

---

## 11. Find the SonarQube Server IP Address

```bash
hostname -I
```

Example:

```text
192.168.1.220
```

Open the following address from a browser:

```text
http://192.168.1.220:9000
```

Replace `192.168.1.220` with the actual IP address of your SonarQube VM.

---

## 12. First Login

Use the default credentials:

```text
Username: admin
Password: admin
```

SonarQube will require you to change the password after the first successful login.

Use a strong password and do not continue using `admin` as the password.

